WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.530
In most video games that we've seen running
for at least a few moments, we learn to anticipate

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.860
what is going to happen in the next second,
and even more, if given the patience and skills,

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.890
we could attempt to recreate parts of the
game itself.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:24.170
And what you see here in this work is actually
even better, because it requires neither the

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.760
patience nor the skills to do that.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.560
So here is the million dollar idea: let's
have a learning algorithm look at some video

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.570
game footage, and then, ask it to recreate
that so we can indulge in playing it.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:42.379
The concept is demonstrated on the Super Mario
game and later, you will also see some results

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:45.590
with the millenial childhood favorite, Mega
Man.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.190
There are many previous works that hook into
the source code of these games and try to

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.719
read and predict what happens next by reading
the code-level instructions.

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:59.460
But not in this case, because this technique
looks at the video output and the learning

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.160
takes place on the level of pixels, therefore,
no access to the inner workings of the game

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.220
is necessary.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:11.220
The algorithm is given two things for the
learning process: one, a sprite pallette that

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.940
contains all the possible elements that can
appear in the game, including landscape tiles,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.370
enemies, coins and so on.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:24.631
And two, we also provide an input video sequence
with one playthrough of the game to demonstrate

00:01:24.631 --> 00:01:28.090
the mechanics and possible interactions between
these game elements.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:33.790
A video is a series of frames, from which
the technique learns how a frame can be advanced

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:34.860
to the next one.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.360
After it has been exposed to enough training
samples, it will be able to do this prediction

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.230
by itself on unknown frames that it hasn't
seen before.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:47.570
This pretty much means that we can start playing
the game that it tries to mimic.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:52.810
And there are similarities across many games
that could be exploited, endowing the learning

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.600
algorithms with knowledge reused from other
games, making them able to recreate even higher

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.320
quality computer games, even in cases where
a given scenario hasn't played out in the

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.530
training footage.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.390
It used to be the privilege of computer graphics
researchers to play video games during work

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:13.530
hours, but apparently, scientists in machine
learning also caught up in this regard.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.530
Way to go!

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:15.930
A word about limitations.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.820
As the predictions are not very speedy and
are based off of a set of facts learned from

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:25.370
the video sequences, it is a question as to
how well this technique would generalize to

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.860
more complex 3d video games.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.760
As almost all research works, this is a stepping
stone, but a very important one at that as

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.100
this is a proof of concept for a really cool
idea.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.450
You know the drill, a couple papers down the
line and we'll see the idea significantly

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:41.450
improved.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:45.930
The results are clearly not perfect, but it
is a nice demonstration of a new concept,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.810
and knowing the rate of progress in machine
learning research, you will very soon see

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.510
some absolutely unreal results.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:58.200
What's even more, I expect that new levels,
enemy types and mechanics will soon be synthesized

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:04.500
to already existing games via generative adversarial
networks, or generative latent optimization.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.290
If you would like to hear more about these,
as always, the links are available in the

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:09.290
video description.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.610
Also, if you enjoyed this episode, please
make sure to help us tell these incredible

